  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n\"The force is strong with that one, Yoda\"...that is the main\ndriver of our current wx pattern...the upper level ridge that \nholds strong thru mid week. As a result, we expect carbon copy \nconditions each day, with highs 75-80F and lows 45-50F \ngenerally over most of the area thru Wednesday. Any patchy \ndaybreak fog that develops will be shallow and short-lived.\n\nWhat happens mid-week with the pattern is not much of a change,\nbut a temporary, subtle weakening in the aforementioned force \nof the upper level ridge as a minor perturbation in the flow\nkinks across the Mid MS Valley. The only tangible impact here \nis seen on time/height cross sections as a manifested increase \nin upper level moisture, i.e. a little spill-over high cloud \nfrom the broader scale Plains troffing as the ridge weakens, \nand more swly flow aloft spills it in/across our area visa vi\nthe perturbation; we're still far removed from tangible \nsensible wx impacts. Temps hold about the same in the upper half\n70s but the increase in cloud cover may hold lows closer to \naround 50F versus the tad cooler mid 40s that exist in some \nlocales earlier in the week.\n\nThe ridge reamplifies for us by the end of the week, as it is\nteleconnectively sandwiched between the primary height falls \nover the Intermountain West, and the cyclone off the Eastern \nSeaboard. Depending upon the exact placement of that ridge, \nwe'll either continue with dry weather or perhaps, it is \ndisplaced just enough to the east that downstream height falls \nfrom the west start to impact more of the MS valley. If that \noccurs, we just might see some small rain chances appear by \nHalloween, but at this writing, it looks to be minimally \nimpactful even if it occurs, as the primary low pressure \ntroffing is still far displaced westward over the MT-WY \nRockies.",
